# Citation information for this lesson, in the Citation File Format (https://citation-file-format.github.io/).
# Keep the author list and the other fields up to date.
# When you first share the lesson, add `version:` and `date-released:` (YYYY-MM-DD).
# Once GitHub Pages serves the lesson, point `url` at the published site
# (for example https://br-johnson.github.io/rtmb-cookbook/).

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "RTMB Cookbook: Fit, Check, Compare and Forecast with Your Own Models"
message: >-
  Please cite this lesson using the information in this file
  when you refer to it in publications, and/or if you
  re-use, adapt, or expand on the content in your own
  training material.
type: dataset
authors:
  # The lesson's Cite page prints only given-names and family-names.
  # family-names repeats the GitHub alias so that the page shows an author;
  # replace it with your given-names and family-names if you want them shown.
  - alias: Br-Johnson
    family-names: Br-Johnson
abstract: >-
  In two hands-on days you will write your own statistical model as a
  short R function, fit it with RTMB, check that the fit worked, compare
  it with other models and test its forecasts on years it never saw,
  using one tested recipe for each step and no proofs.
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/Br-Johnson/rtmb-cookbook"
url: "https://github.com/Br-Johnson/rtmb-cookbook"
keywords:
  - RTMB
  - TMB
  - R
  - statistical modelling
  - likelihood
  - random effects
  - model selection
  - model evaluation
  - forecasting
  - sockeye salmon
  - Carpentries-style lesson
references:
  - type: generic
    title: "RTMB workshop materials, Pacific Salmon Commission, November 2026"
    authors:
      - given-names: Paul
        name-particle: van
        family-names: Dam-Bates
    year: 2026
    month: 11
    institution:
      name: Pacific Salmon Commission
    url: "https://github.com/Pacific-salmon-assess/RTMBWorkshop_Nov2026"
    notes: >-
      Slides, examples and the sockeye salmon data of the two-day RTMB
      workshop hosted by the Pacific Salmon Commission, from which this
      lesson was developed.
